Z||000003||Jay||09-03-2001||09:27 PM||||NOS  the same thing happens to me on import days!  Trailer queens that are full tube chasis end up winning the big bucks, but you're right, alot of people give us looks for our cars and compliments.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||NOS3800||09-03-2001||10:24 PM||||Thanks. The NOS bottle is in the trunk, it is the easiest place to put it. Didn't want to run the lines all the way from front to rear. Besides if it is front I don't think you can keep a spare up there. <BR>Yeah, you will be surprised what gets sponsorships too. There was a 2001 Sentra with CASTROL sponsorship. Car was white with the red and green Castrol logos. It was official cause it came in on a trailer with the logos too. So I watched it run and it BLASTED the 1/4 in 15.5 seconds! What a joke. How does he get sponsored like that? He lost in the semis so he wasn't a professional or anything.||||reg||10
Z||000005||Mickey_Moose||09-03-2001||11:22 PM||||technically if you are racing under 'bracket rules' then it doesn't really matter all that much on horsepower but more on your reaction time and all the little secrets to gaining that little bit more of a lead.<P>example: in your time trials, or don't want to be down the track as quick as you can, run the air and all accessories to eat up some of that horsepower, gives you a longer time, hence a larger head start - the trick is not to cross the line before your estimated. A few people that bracket race all the time use these (and other) tricks to gain a bigger advance at the tree, they just have to let up on the gas a bit at the end so they don't break out.<P>just my 2 cents<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||6
